Application of Symbolic - Numeric Algebra in Geodesy

B. Palancz, P. Zaletnyik, J. Awange, R. H. Lewis*


Department of Photogrammetry and Geoinformatics
Budapest University of Technology and Economy, H - 1521, Hungary

Department of Geodesy and Surveying,
Budapest University of Technology and Economy, H - 1521, Hungary

Western Australian Centre for Geodesy
Curtin University of Technology, Australia

Department of Mathematics
Fordham University , Bronx, NY

Most often geodetic problems are represented as system of =20
multivariable polynomials. The bottleneck in
solving such systems is finding out proper initial values for local =20
iterative numerical methods. If the
problem is relatively modest in size, computer algebraic methods like =20=

Groebner basis or Dixon resultant
can give symbolic solution. To extend this result for overdetermined =20
system one may use Gauss-Jacobi
combinatoric solution employing the symbolic result many times. =20
However, when the number of the
combinatoric subsystems is very high, it is reasonable to employ =20
global, robust numerical method like linear
homotopy. In order to avoid the computation of all possible paths, =20
consequently to simplify homotopy solution,
the initial value of the start system can be evaluated from the =20
symbolic solution of a subsystem.
To illustrate this strategy a real world geodetic problem is =20
presented. The 3 point problem of the 3D affine
transformation is solved with Dixon resultant computed with EDF =20
method. Then employing this result as initial
value of the start system of linear homotopy as numerical method, the =20=

solution is computed for N =3D 1138 points
as well.

