An Attempt to Apply Dixon Resultant to Differential Elimination=03

Lu Yang, Zhenbing Zeng*, Weinian Zhang

Shanghai Key Laboratory of Trustworthy Computing
East China Normal University, Shanghai 200062, P. R. China

Chengdu Institute of Computer Applications
Chinese Academy of Sciences, Chengdu 610041, P. R. China

Yangtze Mathematics Center and Department of Mathematics
Sichuan University, Chengdu 610064, P. R. China

The idea of using resultant for solving differential equations can be =20=

traced to 1930's.
The first work on "differential resultant" was done by O. Ore [19]. =20
Later, the
divisibility and elimination theory of algebraic differential =20
equations was developed by
C. H. Riquier [20], M. Janet [8], J. F. Ritt [21, 22], E. R. Kolchin =20
[12] et al. The notion
of resultant of two nonlinear differential polynomials was introduced =20=

by Ritt [21] in 1932
under some hypothesis on the differential polynomials by using of =20
pseudo-division. In
1997 G. Carra-Ferro [1] introduced a notion of differential resultant =20=

of two ordinary
differential polynomials as quotient of two determinants and proved =20
that a necessary
condition for the existence of a common solution of two algebraic =20
differential equations
is that the differential resultant is equal to zero based on =20
Macaulay's resultant (see
[7, 17, 18]). Methods for using characteristic set theory to solve =20
first order autonomous
ODE and partial difference polynomial systems was propsed by X. S. Gao =20=

et al in [6]. Other
generalizations of differential resultant can be found in [3], [11].
In this paper, we present our joint work on computing differential =20
resultant via
Dixon's resultant [10] for first and higher ODEs. Comparing with the =20
classical resultant
of Sylvester, the advantage of Dixon's resultant is that it can do one-=20=

step elimination of a
block of unknowns from a system of polynomial equations like =20
Macauley's. Meanwhile,
there are works showing that Dixon's resultant is much faster than =20
Macauley's for
certain specific problems (cf. [13, 14, 15, 16]). So the motivation =20
for using Dixon's
resultant to solve differential resultants comes very natural. Our =20
results in this paper
show that this attempt is also worth to develop for many non-trivial =20
problems listed in
E. Kamke's book [9].

